WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.550
The galaxy's scruffiest-looking nerf herder
finally got a film all to himself with Solo:

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:10.390
A Star Wars Story, and as far as all-out,
action-filled heist movies go, it's out of

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:11.060
this world.

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:15.680
Taking place about a decade before Han would
meet Luke and his crew in a dingy little cantina

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.460
on Tatooine, Solo gives us a pretty clear
backstory about the troubled, optimistic,

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:20.960
Han.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.360
Despite the hero's general detachment and
disenchantment from all things Jedi, it's

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:28.860
hard to escape common threads dangling throughout
the Star Wars universe.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:33.080
From name-checked bounty hunters to familiar
face masks, here are a few details you may

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:34.680
have missed in Solo.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:36.900
Stay golden

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:42.180
You'd be blind to not notice Han's gold dice
in Solo, but they have a very confusing history,

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:46.380
and even canonical Star Wars publications
can't seem to agree on what their deal is.

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:49.710
From their first on-screen appearance during
A New Hope, the dice have taken on a life

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:50.380
of their own.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:54.900
Star Wars: The Force Awakens: A Visual Dictionary
includes a picture of the dice: they have

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:59.390
standard pips, and they were allegedly used
in a game of sabacc in which Han won the Falcon

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:00.520
from with Lando Calrissian.

00:01:00.749 --> 00:01:04.820
When they make their re-appearance in The
Last Jedi, their symbols have changed to alien

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:05.320
characters.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:09.160
In Solo, Han hands the dice to Qi'ra before
they're separated on Corellia.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:12.800
The dice are returned to him just before the
crew of the Falcon enter the spice mines of

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:17.400
Kessel, but they definitely aren't used during
Han's sabacc showdown with a cheating Calrissian.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:21.480
They're not even used during Han and Lando's
rematch in which Han actually scores the iconic

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:21.980
ship.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.700
So, like so much of Star Wars lore, we're
going to have to dig just a little deeper

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:29.000
and figure out the real story behind those
Corellian chance cubes.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:31.460
Questions about Kessel

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:36.140
Finally, we get to see what makes the Kessel
Run so dangerous, and why it's such a big

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:40.810
deal that Han Solo made it in less than 12
parsecs, setting to rest the ongoing debate

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:43.860
about a parsec being a unit of distance, not
time.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:48.140
Because of the fancy flying necessary to make
it through the extremely dangerous area of

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:52.540
space, finding a viable shortcut, and surviving
it, is a truly big deal.

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:56.880
Most viewers remember Kessel because of Han's
boasting; the average run takes 20 parsecs,

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.920
but the Falcon made it in 12 - that is, if
you round down.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:04.060
By the time A New Hope rolls around, Han's
story grows, and that number dips to under

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:04.560
12.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.000
During The Force Awakens, Rey believes that
number to be 14 parsecs, which Han quickly

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:09.500
corrects.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:12.220
But that's not even the first time Kessel
is mentioned in Star Wars.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:15.240
The first mention occurs in the first five
minutes of A New Hope

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:16.700
"What are we going to do?

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:18.440
We'll be sent to the spice mines of Kessel!"

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.900
In Solo, we see that Kessel totally sucks.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:26.220
Even though L3-37 staged a pretty successful
robot rebellion in the mines, Star Wars Rebels

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:30.150
: The Visual Guide confirms that the mines
have regular rebellions, all of which seem

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:31.920
to be more or less unsuccessful.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.420
Even a decade later, Kessel still sucks.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:36.580
Big bounty

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.920
Of all of the lives lost in Solo, the definitive
end to a fan favorite bounty hunter was perhaps

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.520
the most surprising, and it didn't even happen
on-screen.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:48.620
Aurra Sing, who made a brief appearance in
The Phantom Menace, went on to appear in both

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:52.820
canonical Star Wars comics and multiple episodes
of The Clone Wars, where she hung out with

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:57.820
Boba Fett and Cad Bane, and even faced down
Jedi warriors Ahsoka Tano and Plo Koon.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:03.040
And then, sometime later, she was "pushed"
by Tobias Beckett, and met her unceremonious

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:03.540
end.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:04.280
That's it.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:07.480
For a once-respected bounty hunter, what a
way to go.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:12.260
Also mentioned in passing is Bossk, the lizard-like
bounty hunter who first appears in The Empire

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:12.960
Strikes Back.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:17.260
Diving even deeper into Star Wars lore, the
"Zan Sisters" are also mentioned at the same

00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:18.270
time as Bossk.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:22.860
While not an obvious riff on Star Wars Legends
continuity, Zan and Zu Pike were a pair of

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:26.360
mercenary sisters who first appeared in Shadows
of the Empire.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:30.930
If there's any doubt about their connection,
consider Teras Kasi, the martial art mentioned

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:34.959
by Qi'ra, which is employed by her, Vos, and
Darth Maul, among others.

00:03:34.959 --> 00:03:39.349
It's first mentioned in Shadows, is used by
the Pike sisters, shows up in the title of

00:03:39.349 --> 00:03:42.940
a terrible video game, and is never mentioned
in canon again until now.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:45.040
Legends

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:49.510
Even though Disney threw out pretty much every
Star Wars comic and book that was written

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:53.910
before they acquired Lucasfilm, much of the
new "canon" continuity of Star Wars is more

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:55.620
or less a remix of the old stuff.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:04:00.180
At one point in Han's original, wiped-out
history, he joined the Empire, didn't fit

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:03.240
in, and eventually saved Chewbacca from the
Empire's abuse.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:04.240
Sound familiar?

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:05.820
Chewie grunt.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:07.300
"That's yes."

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:11.880
"The Maw" also exists in old Star Wars lore,
but it's a cluster of black holes inside the

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:12.560
Kessel Run.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:16.980
The idea is essentially the same in Solo…
plus a horrifying space jellyfish for good

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:17.480
measure.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:22.039
The parallels to things viewers can find in
the discarded history of Star Wars are numerous,

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:25.080
but you'll still never, ever see Mara Jade
on the big screen.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:25.900
Sorry, guys.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:28.140
Crimson Dawn

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:33.300
Dryden Vos, the leader of the crime syndicate
Crimson Dawn, is something of a collector.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:37.819
Keen viewers probably noticed a suit of Mandalorian
armor hanging around in the background of

00:04:37.819 --> 00:04:41.159
his office, which is of course similar to
the suits worn by the Fetts.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:45.280
But it's another curiosity in his office that
probably turned even more heads.

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:49.819
Director Ron Howard announced before the release
of Solo that he'd planted an Indiana Jones

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:54.720
Easter egg in the film, carrying on a long
tradition of the two franchises trading references.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:59.139
Treasures from the Sankara Stones to the fertility
idol are hidden throughout the room, but the

00:04:59.139 --> 00:05:03.919
most obvious treasure is the gigantic crystal
skull that Vos has prominently displayed.

00:05:03.919 --> 00:05:08.789
Vos' skull is definitely more humanoid than
the alien cranium found in Kingdom of the

00:05:08.789 --> 00:05:12.540
Crystal Skull, but it's almost definitely
a reference to the Indiana Jones film.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:14.500
Aboard the Falcon

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:19.180
For decades, all we've really known of the
Millennium Falcon is that it's a temperamental,

00:05:19.189 --> 00:05:22.909
hearty little freighter with a weird shape,
but you might not have even recognized the

00:05:22.909 --> 00:05:23.880
ship in Solo.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:28.339
During Han and Lando's first trip, we see
that the ship is very clean, and also unusually

00:05:28.339 --> 00:05:32.749
teardrop shaped, with a whole lot of additions
made by Lando himself to make it more of a

00:05:32.749 --> 00:05:35.339
pleasure vehicle than one for just serious
business.

00:05:35.339 --> 00:05:39.999
These additions include a cape room, a holochess
table, and some nice sleeping quarters.

00:05:39.999 --> 00:05:43.699
Interestingly, the shape of the old Falcon
actually reflects the original designs of

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:44.980
artist Ralph McQuarrie.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:48.980
Despite all of the damage by the end of Solo,
one thing we know for sure remains aboard

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:53.491
the Falcon is Lando's bounty hunter helmet,
which we first see in Return of the Jedi when

00:05:53.491 --> 00:05:56.879
Lando enters Jabba's palace using the name
Tamtel Skreej.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:59.840
Beckett uses the same helmet to disguise himself
on Kessel.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:02.040
Digging deep

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:07.300
Lando clearly isn't a fan of mining colonies,
but by the time The Empire Strikes Back rolls

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:11.860
around, Calrissian is managing Cloud City,
a Tibanna gas mining colony over Bespin.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:16.460
Keep your eyes peeled on the Falcon and you'll
spot a silver model of the colony among Lando's

00:06:16.469 --> 00:06:17.469
possessions.

00:06:17.469 --> 00:06:21.569
Speaking of clouds, it may also come as a
surprise that Enfys Nest and her Cloud Riders

00:06:21.569 --> 00:06:24.360
actually aren't a completely original creation
for Solo.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:29.920
They're actually inspired by a band of marauders
who appeared in 1978's Star Wars #9, but only

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:30.800
in appearance.

00:06:31.009 --> 00:06:35.520
The comic Cloud-Riders are led by a guy with
the terrible name "Arrogantus," who definitely

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:37.580
isn't into piracy to help the Rebellion.

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:42.520
By then, Marvel's Star Wars comics were already
far off the rails: Jabba the Hut was a skinny

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:47.210
yellow walrus on two legs, while Han was hanging
out with a hedgehog man named Hedji and a

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:50.020
barely-clothed crime boss lady named Amaiza.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:50.920
No thanks.

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:52.980
Weazel and company

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:58.020
Solo doesn't have as many secret cameos as
other recent Star Wars films, but a few are

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:58.960
worth mentioning.

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:03.990
It would be hard to miss the unusual presence
of Clint Howard, brother of director Ron Howard,

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:06.190
and a regular face for fans of science fiction.

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:10.060
You'll spot him as a robot wrangler when we
first meet L3-37.

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:14.920
And while you won't find C-3PO, Solo still
features the Droid's actor, Anthony Daniels,

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:19.150
as a human named Tak, who teams up with the
Wookiees and Chewbacca during the escape from

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:19.650
Kessel.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:24.200
And of course, we have Warwick Davis, who's
been a part of Star Wars since the very beginning,

00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:26.320
most notably appearing as Wicket the Ewok.

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:31.580
Of all of his Star Wars characters, only one
is unmasked: Weazel, a scuzzy spectator at

00:07:31.580 --> 00:07:34.220
the Boonta Eve podrace during The Phantom
Menace.

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:38.960
Davis appears again in Solo as a member the
Cloud-Riders, and once again he's playing

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:39.460
Weazel.

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:43.520
Going from a racing junkie on a dirt planet
to being one of the galaxy's founding freedom

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:47.900
fighters is an arc to parallel anyone else
in Star Wars, and it's an arc we'd love to

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:48.400
see.

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:49.520
Thanks for watching!

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:51.940
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:54.040
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

